Shortcut Trick
For **supplementary angles**, the sum is always 180&deg;.

Let the angles be L (larger) and S (smaller).

L + S = 180&deg; and L &minus; S = 60&deg;

**Smaller Angle (S)** = (Sum &minus; Difference) &divide; 2

S = (180&deg; &minus; 60&deg;) &divide; 2 = 120&deg; &divide; 2 = 60&deg;

**&there4; The correct answer is 60&deg;.**

**Alternate Method**

**Given:**

Sum of **supplementary angles** = 180&deg;

Difference between the two angles = 60&deg;

**Formula Used:**

Sum of angles on a **straight line** = 180&deg;

**Calculations:**

Let the **smaller angle** be x.

According to the question, the **larger angle** = x + 60&deg;

&rArr; x + (x + 60&deg;) = 180&deg;

